WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.623
Every multicellular organism faces the same five problems.

2
00:00:08.363 --> 00:00:09.763
Get energy and materials.

3
00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:12.603
Exchange and move them, including wastes.

4
00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:15.363
Keep its inside relatively stable.

5
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:17.623
Resist damage and invasion.

6
00:00:18.423 --> 00:00:19.423
And leave descendants.

7
00:00:20.403 --> 00:00:23.603
This checklist connects a barley plant, a honeybee and a human.

8
00:00:24.563 --> 00:00:26.083
Every solution carries a cost.

9
00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:31.503
To understand a body, we need to ask how it solves these problems, and how its

10
00:00:31.503 --> 00:00:32.503
solutions evolved.

11
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.253
Plants capture light and absorb water and minerals through their roots.

12
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:41.133
Materials must reach every cell, and wastes must leave.

13
00:00:42.093 --> 00:00:45.513
Homeostasis keeps the internal environment relatively stable.

14
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.233
Defense resists damage and invasion.

15
00:00:49.253 --> 00:00:50.833
Reproduction continues a lineage.

16
00:00:51.813 --> 00:00:56.393
A honeybee worker can help pass on copies of its genes by raising close relatives.

17
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.847
A barley leaf shows the cost of exchange.

18
00:01:01.047 --> 00:01:03.907
Carbon dioxide enters through tiny pores called stomata.

19
00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:06.927
Water vapour leaves through those same pores.

20
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.507
When the stomata are open, both things happen at once.

21
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.480
Your lungs face a similar bargain.

22
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.320
Gas must dissolve in a wet film before entering cells.

23
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.060
A wet surface in air loses water.

24
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.300
In ordinary, drier air, each breath carries water away.

25
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.840
Neither solution escapes the cost.

26
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:31.993
Water-use efficiency is carbon gained divided by water lost.

27
00:01:32.873 --> 00:01:34.993
Here are made-up leaves, not barley data.

28
00:01:35.993 --> 00:01:43.013
Leaf A gains 20 µmol of CO2 and loses 5 mmol of water, per m² per s.

29
00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:45.673
Its efficiency is 4.0.

30
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:49.187
Leaf B gains 16 and loses 3.

31
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:51.807
Its efficiency is 5.3.

32
00:01:52.707 --> 00:01:55.927
When water is scarce, B keeps more water per unit of carbon.

33
00:01:56.827 --> 00:02:00.427
When water is plentiful and carbon limits growth, A gains more.

34
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.310
Why do structures fit what they do?

35
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.790
Natural selection acts on inherited variation.

36
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:12.490
Over generations, traits linked to greater reproductive success can become more

37
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:15.190
common. But the fit is never perfect.

38
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.610
Trade-offs link gains to costs.

39
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.470
Selection works with inherited structures.

40
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.670
Some changes spread by chance, and some features are by-products.

41
00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.570
Evolution has no preconceived ideal.

42
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.930
We also need complementary questions.

43
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.950
Mechanism asks how guard cells open and close a pore.

44
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.390
Development asks how genes set the number of stomata.

45
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.490
Adaptive significance asks how density affects survival and reproduction.

46
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.090
History asks how density changed across a lineage.

47
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.490
The first pair concerns an organism’s lifetime.

48
00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.930
The second concerns evolution.

49
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.170
A full account needs both.
